 ## Overview
-1D Transfer Function Generation and Sampling maps scalar volume data values to RGBA color and opacity properties using a one-dimensional lookup table (LUT) [S1, book]. It solves the problem of quantitative data visualization by converting numerical voxel intensities into visual appearance (color and transparency) [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Parametric Function Generation](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) which computes values analytically, or [Color Blending](5150-color-blending.md) which composites final pixels, this method performs a deterministic, per-voxel assignment based on a pre-computed 1D LUT [S1, book]. It builds on [Lookup Table Implementation](5347-lookup-table-implementation.md) by defining the specific algorithm for constructing the LUT from user-defined control points and sampling it during rendering.
+1D Transfer Function Generation and Sampling maps scalar volume data values to RGBA color and opacity properties using a one-dimensional lookup table (LUT) [S1, book]. It solves the problem of quantitative data visualization by assigning visual appearance to numerical voxel intensities [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Parametric Function Generation](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) which computes values analytically, or [Color Blending](5150-color-blending.md) which composites final pixels, this method performs a deterministic, per-voxel assignment based on a pre-computed 1D LUT [S1, book]. It builds on [Lookup Table Implementation](5347-lookup-table-implementation.md) by defining the specific algorithm for constructing the LUT from user-defined control points and sampling it during rendering.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of control points defining the transfer function. Each control point consists of a scalar value (position) and an RGBA color vector. Optional inputs include Gaussian opacity objects or separate alpha control points [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** A 1D texture (LUT) containing $N$ entries of RGBA values, typically stored as a contiguous array of 4-component vectors [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A set of control points defining the transfer function. Each control point consists of a scalar value (position) and an RGBA color vector [S5, unofficial][S9, unofficial]. Optional inputs include Gaussian opacity objects [S9, unofficial] or separate alpha control points [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A contiguous array of 4-component vectors (RGBA) of size $N$ (typically a power of two, e.g., 256, 1024, 65536) [S1, book][S2, unofficial][S9, unofficial]. This array is uploaded to the GPU as a 1D texture (or 2D texture with height 1) for sampling [S2, unofficial][S9, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures**
-The LUT is stored as a contiguous array of size $N$ (where $N$ is a power of two, e.g., 256, 1024, 4096) [S1, book][S9, unofficial].
-*   **Format:** Typically `float4` (32-bit floats per component) or 8-bit integers (`uint8_t[4]`) [S1, book][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Layout:** A 1D array where index $i$ corresponds to the normalized value mapping to entry $i$. In some hardware implementations, the LUT may be 4096 entries deep with 36-bit entries (8-bit RGB, 12-bit alpha) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Control Point Structure:**
-    *   `ControlPoint`: Fields `value` (scalar/int), `color` (TFColor).
-    *   `TFColor`: Fields `r`, `g`, `b`, `a` (all float, range [0.0, 1.0]) [S5, unofficial].
-    *   `AlphaControl`: Fields `pos` (vec2f) where `pos.x` is value and `pos.y` is alpha [S9, unofficial].
-
